item.text:
Wave 1 ledger: about 44 candidates surfaced, 20 opened, 8 duplicates/near-duplicates, 3 irrelevant/out-of-period, 1 inaccessible, 12 accepted.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Expansions to Child Tax Credit Contributed to 46% Decline in Child Poverty Since 2020","publisher":"U.S. Census Bureau","publication_date":"2022-09-13","url":"https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/09/record-drop-in-child-poverty.html","claim":"Census reported that child poverty measured by the Supplemental Poverty Measure fell to a record low 5.2% in 2021 and said the expanded Child Tax Credit materially contributed to the decline.","locator":"lines 166-181 and 207-209","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary federal statistical release with precise target-period outcome data and explicit policy attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to Biden-era social-welfare effects in the 2021-2022 policy window; retrospective to 2021 but published in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong: the effect is tied to ARPA/CTC expansions advanced under Biden and implemented by federal agencies.","contrary_evidence":["Official poverty fell only 0.7 percentage points.","Durability was limited because the expansion later expired."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Next steps on the Child Tax Credit","publisher":"Brookings","publication_date":"2022-11-28","url":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/next-steps-on-the-child-tax-credit/","claim":"Brookings noted that the American Rescue Plan Child Tax Credit expansion had ended, leaving nearly 19 million of the poorest children with no or only a partial credit and showing the gains were not durable without extension.","locator":"line 209","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent policy analysis that directly evaluates the distributional durability of the 2021-2022 child-welfare gains.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 analysis of the post-expansion welfare environment; relevant as a durability caveat for target-year gains.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the policy design and lapse under Biden-era conditions, but this source is mainly a follow-on durability critique rather than a fresh 2022 delivery action.","contrary_evidence":["The same Brookings line acknowledges the earlier ARPA expansion sharply reduced child poverty.","This is a follow-on critique, not a contemporaneous delivery record."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden-Harris Administration Announces 14.5 Million Americans Signed Up for Affordable Health Care During Historic Open Enrollment Period","publisher":"Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services","publication_date":"2022-01-27","url":"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/press-releases/biden-harris-administration-announces-14-5-million-americans-signed-up-affordable-health-care-during","claim":"CMS said 14.5 million people signed up for 2022 Marketplace coverage and that 5.8 million people newly gained coverage since the start of the Biden-Harris Administration.","locator":"line 61 and line 73","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary agency release with a dated enrollment outcome and explicit administration attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 coverage outcome, though the enrollment season spans late 2021 into early 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strong but mediated through ACA subsidies and CMS outreach; Biden publicly framed the action and the underlying subsidies.","contrary_evidence":["Enrollment gains were partly subsidy-driven rather than a pure delivery metric.","The release is enrollment, not health outcome, data."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biden-Harris Administration Launches 10th Year of Marketplace Open Enrollment with Four Out of Five Customers Eligible for Coverage at $10 or Less, Thanks to Subsidies","publisher":"Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services","publication_date":"2022-10-31","url":"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/press-releases/biden-harris-administration-launches-10th-year-marketplace-open-enrollment-four-out-five-customers","claim":"CMS said four out of five Marketplace customers could find plans for $10 or less per month after tax credits during the 2022 open-enrollment period.","locator":"lines 66-71","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official statement with a concrete affordability metric and policy linkage to Biden-signed subsidies.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 open-enrollment evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Biden-backed ACA/IRA subsidy policy and CMS implementation, not solely to the president personally.","contrary_evidence":["This is premium affordability, not total out-of-pocket cost.","Actual plan uptake still depends on consumer choice and state-marketplace participation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"As American Families Prepare for Back-to-School, Biden-Harris Administration Strengthens Access to High-Quality, Comprehensive Health Care for Children","publisher":"Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services","publication_date":"2022-08-18","url":"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/press-releases/american-families-prepare-back-school-biden-harris-administration-strengthens-access-high-quality","claim":"CMS said HHS issued guidance to expand behavioral-health coverage for children in Medicaid, expand school-based health care, and standardize quality measures to improve children's access to care.","locator":"lines 60-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary administrative guidance with detailed implementation actions and explicit child-health targets.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022 implementation guidance.","ruler_attribution":"Direct federal action under Biden through HHS and CMS; execution depended on state implementation.","contrary_evidence":["These were guidance and proposed rules, not realized outcomes.","State uptake and quality gains were still pending."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"HHS Approves Nation’s First Medicaid Mobile Crisis Intervention Services Program, To Be Launched in Oregon","publisher":"Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services","publication_date":"2022-09-12","url":"https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/press-releases/hhs-approves-nations-first-medicaid-mobile-crisis-intervention-services-program-be-launched-oregon","claim":"CMS approved Oregon's Medicaid mobile-crisis proposal, allowing community-based stabilization services for people in mental-health or substance-use crises with 24/7 specialist access.","locator":"lines 59-60","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary 2022 program approval with a specific service design and target population.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 crisis-care policy action.","ruler_attribution":"Direct federal policy action under Biden via HHS/CMS; state execution was required for delivery.","contrary_evidence":["Initial rollout was state-specific, not national.","The release announces approval, not measured outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.6","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rural Maternity and Obstetrics Management Strategies Program","publisher":"Health Resources and Services Administration","publication_date":"2022-01-05","url":"https://www.hrsa.gov/grants/find-funding/HRSA-22-115","claim":"HRSA's RMOMS notice said the program was intended to improve access to and continuity of maternal and obstetric care in rural communities and to improve maternal and neonatal outcomes.","locator":"lines 127-130","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary grant notice specifying target services, target population, and intended health outcomes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 program design and funding opportunity.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Biden-era HHS/HRSA policy, but actual outcomes depended on grantees and later implementation.","contrary_evidence":["This is a funding opportunity, not realized service delivery.","Outcome evidence is not contained in the notice."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"USDA Extends Flexibility that’s Helping Manufacturers, States get Formula to WIC Families","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture","publication_date":"2022-10-13","url":"https://www.usda.gov/about-usda/news/press-releases/2022/10/13/usda-extends-flexibility-thats-helping-manufacturers-states-get-formula-wic-families","claim":"USDA extended WIC funding flexibility that let state agencies and formula manufacturers provide more formula options to WIC families and covered the added cost of non-contract formula.","locator":"line 460","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary agency action with a concrete crisis-response mechanism and direct family-benefit pathway.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 infant-formula crisis response.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Biden-administration action through USDA, though it was a temporary response to a supply crisis.","contrary_evidence":["The measure was temporary, extended only through December 2022.","It addressed supply disruption rather than a durable nutrition-system redesign."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2022: Effect on Child Nutrition Programs","publisher":"U.S. Department of Agriculture, Food and Nutrition Service","publication_date":"2022-04-04","url":"https://www.fns.usda.gov/cn/consolidated-appropriations-act-2022","claim":"USDA said the 2022 appropriations act gave school meal programs added flexibility and that, beginning July 1, 2022, transitional meal-pattern standards allowed low-fat flavored milk in school meals and related child nutrition programs.","locator":"lines 111-121","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary program memo with specific statutory implementation details and dates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly target-year 2022 child-nutrition implementation.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but real federal administrative action under Biden through USDA implementation of enacted appropriations.","contrary_evidence":["Mostly menu flexibility and reimbursement mechanics rather than outcome data.","The memo does not by itself show realized nutrition gains."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Data Shows 11% Decline in Veteran Homelessness Since 2020—the Biggest Drop in 5+ Years","publisher":"United States Interagency Council on Homelessness","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://usich.gov/news-events/news/new-data-shows-11-decline-veteran-homelessness-2020-biggest-drop-5-years","claim":"USICH said the 2022 Point-in-Time Count showed an 11% decline in veteran homelessness since early 2020, the biggest drop in more than five years.","locator":"line 84","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary interagency release with a quantified target-year outcome and clear attribution chain.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 outcome on a vulnerable population.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed by USICH to the Biden-era VA/HUD/USICH effort, though the result also reflects local systems and the pandemic baseline.","contrary_evidence":["This covers veterans only, not homelessness overall.","The count was a two-year interval because the 2021 count was incomplete."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Emergency Rental Assistance Helped Prevent Evictions, But Oversight of Payments Was Limited","publisher":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","publication_date":"2022-12-20","url":"https://www.gao.gov/blog/emergency-rental-assistance-prevented-evictions-oversight-payments-was-limited","claim":"GAO said the Emergency Rental Assistance program helped prevent evictions and housing instability, while also noting oversight weaknesses.","locator":"line 40","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent federal oversight source that directly addresses both benefit and implementation limits.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 housing-stability evidence, using the pandemic-response program still active under Biden.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly indirect because the program was congressionally funded and administered through Treasury, but the Biden administration was responsible for implementation and oversight choices.","contrary_evidence":["GAO also warned about payment oversight and data limitations.","This is pandemic-response aid rather than a Biden-created program from scratch."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Remarks on the Federal Student Loan Debt Relief Program and an Exchange With Reporters","publisher":"The American Presidency Project","publication_date":"2022-10-17","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/remarks-the-federal-student-loan-debt-relief-program-and-exchange-with-reporters","claim":"Biden launched the student-loan relief application, said it was simple and available in English and Spanish, and said more than 40 million Americans could benefit, with about 90% of the benefit going to people making under $75,000.","locator":"lines 65-75 and 90-90","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential remarks with concrete distributional claims and a clear implementation milestone.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year 2022 policy launch and rollout.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential action and messaging, though legal authority and agency execution were necessary for delivery.","contrary_evidence":["The program was still at the application stage in 2022.","Implementation and legal durability were contested."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Student Loans: Education Should Proactively Manage Fraud Risks in Any Future Debt Relief Efforts","publisher":"U.S. Government Accountability Office","publication_date":"2023-11-16","url":"https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-24-107142","claim":"GAO found Education approved borrowers in the student-loan debt-relief program without fully evaluating the accuracy and outcomes of its application process and recommended stronger fraud-risk management in any future relief effort.","locator":"lines 297-300","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent oversight report with specific process critique and a direct recommendation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective on a 2022 Biden program; useful as a limitation on implementation quality.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is to Biden-era Education policy design and execution rather than a personal act by Biden alone.","contrary_evidence":["GAO is evaluating process quality, not denying the welfare intent of the program.","The report is post-period and concerns future-effort controls as much as the 2022 launch."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
